.TH FLOW 2
.SH NAME
flow: new, setvelocity, step, maxstep \- incompressible flow in two dimensions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "flow.m";
flow := load Flow Flow->PATH;
Sim: import flow;

flow->init();
(s, err) := flow->new(nx, ny, lx, ly, visc, Flow->PERIODIC);
flow->setvelocity(s, f);
err = flow->step(s, dt);
(u, v) := s.at(x, y);
d := s.divergence();
dt := flow->maxstep(s);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Flow
solves the Navier-Stokes equations for a fluid that cannot be
compressed, in two dimensions, by Chorin's projection method on a
staggered grid.
.PP
.B New
makes a box
.I lx
by
.I ly
divided into
.I nx
by
.I ny
cells, with kinematic viscosity
.IR visc .
The edges are
.B PERIODIC
(both directions wrap),
.B CHANNEL
(wraps left to right, walls top and bottom) or
.B CAVITY
(walls all round).
.B Sim.lid
is how fast the top wall slides, for a channel or a cavity;
.B Sim.fx
and
.B Sim.fy
are a uniform body force.
.PP
.B Setvelocity
sets the initial field from a function of position.
Each component is sampled where it lives and not at the cell centre,
which is the whole point of the arrangement below.
.PP
.B Step
advances by
.IR dt .
.B Maxstep
is the largest step that is stable, for advection and for diffusion
whichever binds; a step far beyond it is refused rather than answered
with numbers that are about to become infinite, and
.B Sim.courant
gives the ratio for a proposed step.
.SS The staggered grid
Pressure lives at cell centres and each velocity component on the faces
it points through.
That is not a detail.
Putting everything at the centres instead is the obvious arrangement and
it is wrong: it leaves the pressure free to oscillate from cell to cell
without the velocity noticing \- the chequerboard mode \- and a solution
full of it looks perfectly smooth in the velocity and is nonsense in the
pressure.
.PP
Because of it, no array holds both components at one place.
.B Sim.at
interpolates each from where it lives, which is the only honest way to
ask for the velocity at a point.
.SS The projection
A step is taken ignoring the pressure, which leaves a velocity that does
not conserve mass.
The pressure is then whatever must be subtracted to make it do so, and
finding it is a Poisson equation.
That system
.I is
symmetric positive definite \- unlike
.IR circuit (2)'s,
which is why that one is solved directly \- so it goes to
.IR krylov (2)'s
conjugate gradients, matrix-free.
.PP
.B Sim.divergence
is the largest divergence anywhere.
After a step it is the projection's own residual and belongs at
rounding; it sits at about 1e\-15 in
.IR flowtest (1).
If it drifts upward, mass is being made.
.PP
With every edge periodic the pressure is determined only up to a
constant and the Poisson operator is singular.
That is the physics \- only the gradient of pressure appears in the
equations \- and the mean is removed from the right-hand side and the
answer.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/flow.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR krylov (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR circuit (2),
.IR flowtest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B New
and
.B step
return a diagnostic string, nil on success: a grid too small to have an
interior, a box of no size, a negative viscosity, a step far beyond the
stable one, a pressure solve that did not converge.
.SH BUGS
Time stepping is first order \- the momentum step is forward Euler and
the projection is Chorin's original, which commits a splitting error of
the same order.
The scheme is second order in space, so a run that halves the spacing
should also shorten the step to keep the two balanced.
.PP
Advection is by a centred difference, which is second order and has no
numerical dissipation at all.
That is why it is right for the smooth flows here and wrong for a
sharp one: at a high Reynolds number it produces oscillations cell by
cell rather than the smearing an upwind scheme would give, and there is
no upwind option.
.PP
Two dimensions only, one fluid, constant density and viscosity, no
temperature, no free surface, no obstacle inside the box.
The only inflow is a body force or a sliding lid.
.PP
The mean removed from the pressure right-hand side is, for all three
edge conditions here, only rounding: the divergences telescope to the
flux through the boundary, which is zero by construction.
Deleting it changes no answer in
.IR flowtest (1).
It is kept because the next boundary condition added may not telescope.
